LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Performance: the conversation with a record", "13", "<p>The track's first four modules governed presence; this one governs contribution — and its thesis holds two ideas in one sentence: <b>performance is a conversation with a record.</b> Drop either half and the machinery fails in its own characteristic way.</p><p><b>The two failure modes.</b> <b>No performance management</b> — the commoner disease in the trade: no goals, no reviews, no record; contribution assessed by impression, promotion by proximity (the person the owner sees most rises fastest — module 1's founder-bottleneck breeding its succession the same way), correction by explosion (nothing said for months, then everything at once), and the good quiet performer invisible until they resign. <b>Performance theatre</b> — the corporate import gone hollow: forms filled annually because the calendar says so, ratings assigned from a fortnight's memory, documents filed and never referenced, the whole exercise costing days and changing nothing — the appraisal as ritual without a reader, which is this academy's definition of waste (every ritual it has ever installed had a read and a consequence). The cure is the thesis's two halves working together: the CONVERSATION continuous (feedback near events, check-ins on a cadence — Chapter 6), and the RECORD periodic and evidence-based (the appraisal that references what the year actually shows — Chapters 3-5), each keeping the other honest: conversation without record evaporates; record without conversation ambushes.</p><p><b>The never-ambush law, promoted.</b> Module 3 taught it for attendance; this module makes it the performance layer's constitution: <b>nothing in an appraisal should be news.</b> The rating that surprises, the weakness first named in the annual meeting, the goal the person learns they missed after the year ends — each is a failure of the continuous half, and each converts a development instrument into a grievance generator. The test the consultant teaches managers: before the appraisal meeting, ask <i>has everything on this form already been said, closer to when it happened?</i> — and if not, the form is not ready, the manager is.</p><p><b>What this module is for, honestly.</b> The performance layer's products, named so the machinery stays aimed: DEVELOPMENT first (the person who knows where they stand and what growth looks like — module 6 consumes this module's outputs directly), DECISIONS second (the confirmation, the promotion, the increment recommendation, the improvement plan — each better made from records than impressions, with the money computations themselves living across the payroll boundary as always), and FAIRNESS throughout (the quiet performer made visible by evidence; the connected performer measured by the same scale — the pipeline's structured-interview argument, replayed annually for everyone already inside). What it is NOT for: surveillance theatre, rank-and-punish tournaments, or the imported bell curve applied to an eleven-person firm — the sizing honesty that has closed every thesis chapter closes this one: the machinery below fits a Nigerian trading firm's scale, and its heaviest instrument is a conversation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Goals & KRAs: what good looks like, written down", "13", "<p>The performance conversation needs a subject, and the subject is set at the year's start: the GOALS — the written answer to <i>what does good look like in this role, this year?</i> The objective-setting chapter.</p><p><b>Goals from the role's actual work.</b> The design source is the academy's own curriculum: the role's key result areas (KRAs) derive from what the role actually does and what the firm can actually observe — the till assistant's (session accuracy, the clean-close rate, service conduct), the storekeeper's (receiving discipline, count accuracy, the custody record), the collections officer's (the aging worked, the promises kept), the accountant's (the close's timeliness, the reconciliations' residue ages), the branch manager's (the branch scorecard's vitals — Inventory 6's page becoming a person's objectives). The insight that makes this trade's performance management CHEAPER than the corporate import: four tracks already built the measurements — the operational reads exist, are captured by machinery, and update without anyone filling a form — so the KRA layer largely SELECTS from evidence that already flows rather than inventing metrics that need feeding (Chapter 3's whole argument, previewed).</p><p><b>The goal disciplines.</b> Probation's law (module 2), generalised to everyone: goals are FEW (three to five — the objective sheet with twelve goals prioritises nothing), OBSERVABLE (tied to evidence the year will actually produce — the goal nobody can measure becomes a mood at appraisal time), SHARED (the person holds the same sheet the manager holds, from the setting conversation — the appraisal against secret criteria is the trial with secret charges), and WEIGHTED where roles need it (the mix of operational KRAs and development goals — module 6's training path entering the sheet as a goal, which is how development stops being optional). Plus the honest balance the trade requires: goals include the QUALITATIVE where the role's value is qualitative (the service conduct, the team contribution — assessed by described behaviours, not invented numbers: a fake metric is worse than a frank judgment, because the judgment admits what it is).</p><p><b>Cascading, lightly.</b> The firm's year shapes the sheets without drowning them: the firm-level goals (the year's few — the Akure ramp, the close's day-four target, the shrinkage band) cascade as CONTEXT and, where genuinely applicable, as shared objectives (the branch's team sharing the branch's vitals — the scorecard already published per module 6 of Inventory; the goal sheet simply points at it), and the cascade stops where relevance stops: the till assistant does not carry the firm's expansion goal; they carry the counter's excellence, which IS their contribution to it. The one-page rule closes the chapter: a person's year fits on a page — the goals, the weights, the evidence sources, the signatures of the setting conversation — and that page is the appraisal's skeleton, written in January so December can only confirm what the year already said.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The evidence layer: appraisals that reference records", "14", "<p>The chapter that makes this module the academy's own: the appraisal's evidence is largely ALREADY CAPTURED — by the machinery four tracks installed — and the performance layer's job is to organise it per person, not to invent it. The evidence chapter.</p><p><b>The inventory of existing evidence.</b> What the systems already know, per role, without a single performance form: the TILL roles — session records (clean closes, variances, the streaks module 2's probation already used), service interactions where captured; the WAREHOUSE roles — receiving records (the count-before-sign discipline visible in every receipt's trail), cycle-count accuracy, custody counts signed, the adjustment history (the storekeeper whose locations count clean is holding a provable standard); the DRIVER roles — the tailgate reconciliations, the run records, the seal history; the SALES roles — the attributed revenue, the margin quality, the credit outcomes of their customers (the selling track's reads per person); the FINANCE roles — the close's lines owned and their timeliness, reconciliation residue ages, correction-log entries by author (Accounts 2's aggregate tells, read per person as craft evidence); and EVERYONE — the attendance layer (module 3's rates, as context: one stream, never the whole story), the training record (module 6's completions and scores — the academy's own certifications as performance evidence), and the conduct file (recognitions and documented conversations — Chapter 6).</p><p><b>The evidence folder, accumulated.</b> The per-person organisation: through the year, the evidence ACCUMULATES against the goal sheet (the manager's log — Chapter 6 — plus the operational reads' periodic snapshots: the quarter's session summary attached, the count-accuracy read filed), so the appraisal is ASSEMBLED, not composed — the December meeting opens a folder that already tells the year's story, and the writing is synthesis, not archaeology. The economics the consultant sells: this is the audit-file argument (Accounts 7) applied to people — the system that runs well evidences well, and the firm already paying for operational discipline gets its performance evidence free.</p><p><b>Recency and halo, killed by records.</b> The two memory diseases every un-evidenced appraisal carries, named so the cure is understood: RECENCY (the year rated by its last six weeks — the strong November erasing the weak spring, or the one December error erasing a strong year) and HALO (the general impression colouring every specific rating — the likeable person scored high on accuracy nobody checked). Both die the same death: the folder's evidence is dated and spans the year, so the rating conversation reads the YEAR — the quarterly snapshots outvoting the last fortnight's impression — and disagreements resolve by opening records instead of comparing recollections (the drill-before-disputing culture of Accounts 1, at the appraisal table).</p><p><b>The evidence boundary.</b> The discipline that keeps the layer trusted: evidence is used WITH the person's knowledge (the goal sheet named its sources in January — nobody is surprised in December that their sessions were read, because the reading was the deal), CONTEXTUALISED (the variance rate of the till that handles triple the traffic is read against its volume; the count accuracy of the location with the hardest stock is read against its difficulty — evidence without context is a number pretending to be a judgment), and BALANCED (the operational reads measure what they measure; the qualitative half of the role gets its qualitative assessment — the machine's numbers inform the conversation and never replace it). The layer's one-sentence constitution: <b>records make the appraisal honest; the manager makes it fair.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The appraisal cycle: the machinery", "13", "<p>The periodic record's machinery: the cycle defined, the document structured, the ratings calibrated, and the meeting run as a conversation. The cycle chapter.</p><p><b>The cycle.</b> The working norm for the trade: the ANNUAL appraisal (the full record — goals reviewed, evidence assembled, ratings given, the development plan written) with the MID-YEAR check (the lighter formal touchpoint — on track or course-correcting, the goal sheet amended where the year changed it: the mid-probation review's logic, annualised for everyone), riding the continuous layer of Chapter 6 beneath both. The calendar discipline: the cycle's dates fixed and published (module 8's calendar), the season bounded (appraisals completed within the stated window — the season that drags to March is rating a year nobody remembers), and the sequence deliberate (self-assessment first, then the manager's, then the meeting — each stage feeding the next).</p><p><b>The appraisal document.</b> The structure, section by section: the GOAL REVIEW (each goal from January's sheet: the evidence, the outcome, the rating — the skeleton filled), the SELF-ASSESSMENT (the person's own account first — because the self-assessment read before the manager writes theirs surfaces the perception gaps early, and the person who rates themselves honestly low on a real gap has started the development conversation themselves), the MANAGER'S ASSESSMENT (evidence-referenced, specific, written in the language of behaviours and results — <i>the receiving discipline held on all forty-one receipts sampled</i> beats <i>good worker</i> in every way that matters later), the OVERALL RATING (Chapter 5's discipline), and the DEVELOPMENT PLAN (the forward half — the growth areas, the training path additions, module 6's intake: an appraisal without a development plan is a verdict without a sentence, and development is the sentence this system wants).</p><p><b>Calibration: ratings consistent across managers.</b> The multi-manager firm's quiet quality machinery, at trade scale: before ratings finalise, the managers' proposed ratings are REVIEWED TOGETHER (the calibration session — HR convening, the spine's managers present: each manager's distribution visible beside the others', the outliers discussed against evidence), because uncalibrated ratings measure the MANAGER (the lenient one whose team is uniformly outstanding; the harsh one whose identical team is uniformly average), and every downstream decision — increments, promotions, the improvement conversations — inherits the distortion. The session's rules: evidence talks (the challenged rating defends itself from the folder, not the podium), the goal is CONSISTENCY not a forced curve (the bell curve imposed on an eleven-person firm manufactures injustice to satisfy a template — the academy's sizing honesty, again: small teams can genuinely be mostly good, and the calibration confirms it rather than denying it), and the session's adjustments are made BEFORE meetings, so the person hears one message, once.</p><p><b>The meeting.</b> The conversation the whole cycle exists to enable: scheduled properly (time protected, privacy real), structured as a dialogue (the self-assessment discussed, the evidence walked, the rating explained — not a form read aloud at a silent listener), forward-weighted (the development plan getting the meeting's second half — the past is the evidence; the future is the point), and closed with signatures and the shared record (the person's comments field theirs to use, including disagreement — the appraisal they can annotate is the appraisal they can trust). The never-ambush test applied one final time at the door: if any line of the form is news, the meeting is happening a year late.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Ratings discipline: the scale & its honesty", "13", "<p>The rating is the appraisal's most consequential compression — a year into a mark — and this chapter is the discipline that keeps the mark meaning something. The scale chapter.</p><p><b>The scale, anchored.</b> The firm's scale (the common five points serve) defined with ANCHORS — each point a described standard, not a number with a vibe: what 'exceeds' looks like in evidence (the goals surpassed with the records to show it), what 'meets' means (the year delivered as the sheet defined — the anchor the consultant defends hardest, because 'meets' must be an honourable rating in a healthy system: the solid performer who did exactly what good looks like is the firm's backbone, and a culture where 'meets' reads as failure inflates every rating above it within two cycles), what the development points mean (the gap named, the plan attached), and what the lowest means (the improvement process engaged — Chapter 7). The anchors are published — everyone rates and is rated against the same descriptions — and the goal sheet's evidence sources make each anchor testable.</p><p><b>The rating diseases, named.</b> The distortions every rating system breeds, taught so managers recognise their own: CENTRAL TENDENCY (everything a middle rating — the manager avoiding both the difficult conversation and the increment argument, producing a distribution that says nothing), LENIENCY (everything high — kindness in the moment, cruelty at the system level: the inflated team's genuine star is indistinguishable from its passengers, the underperformer ambushed years later by a file of 'outstanding' ratings that make the honest conversation legally awkward — the leniency that feels kind is the ambush's co-author), HALO and its inverse (Chapter 3's memory disease, at rating time), and the SPILL diseases (the attendance blemish bleeding into the craft rating; the personality friction rating the work — the scale rates the goal sheet, and the goal sheet named its subjects). The calibration session (Chapter 4) is where the diseases meet daylight: distributions compared, anchors re-read, evidence opened.</p><p><b>The distribution read.</b> HR's post-season read, at altitude: the ratings' shape per team and per manager (the all-outstanding team is a leniency flag or a genuine unit — the evidence folder says which; the no-development-points team suggests avoidance; the year-on-year drift maps inflation), read as QUESTIONS for next season's calibration, never as quotas for this one — the read improves the raters, not the numbers.</p><p><b>Decoupled but connected: ratings and money.</b> The boundary discipline that keeps both systems honest: the rating FEEDS decisions (the increment recommendation, the promotion case, the improvement process — each citing the appraisal), but the money computations live where money lives (the increment matrix, the bonus arithmetic — the payroll boundary and the owner's budget), and the appraisal meeting is NOT a salary negotiation (the meeting that becomes one stops being a development conversation permanently — the two conversations scheduled apart, deliberately, so the rating can be honest without being a bid). And the WRITTEN RATIONALE closes the chapter's discipline: every rating carries its paragraph — the evidence cited, the anchor referenced — because the rating that cannot explain itself in writing is an impression wearing a number, and every process this rating will ever feed (the promotion panel, the improvement plan, the dispute) will ask for the paragraph the lazy rating didn't write.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The continuous layer: feedback, recognition & the log", "13", "<p>The year between appraisals is where performance actually happens — and the continuous layer is the conversation half of the thesis, structured lightly enough to survive real management. The everyday chapter.</p><p><b>Feedback near events.</b> The layer's first law: feedback lands CLOSE to what it concerns — the correction at week one (the receiving shortcut named the week it appeared: a two-minute conversation and a habit adjusted) versus the same correction at month twelve (an appraisal grievance and a habit calcified) — and the arithmetic the consultant teaches managers is the escalation-path economics of module 3, generalised: early feedback is cheap and effective; deferred feedback is expensive and resented, and the deferral is usually the manager's discomfort, not the person's unreadiness. The craft notes that make it land: specific (the behaviour, the instance, the impact — not the character), private for correction and public for recognition (the asymmetry that builds trust), and two-directional (the manager who asks <i>what should I do differently?</i> earns the right to be heard on everything else).</p><p><b>Recognition captured.</b> The record's positive half — chronically under-built in firms that only document problems: the recognition RECORDED (the clean-audit quarter, the caught fraud attempt — Inventory's seal forensics finding its heroes, the customer's compliment, the colleague's cover during the maternity leave), lightly (a dated note to the file — the machinery is a text field, not a ceremony), and deliberately (the manager's monthly discipline: what went RIGHT this month, per person, noted) — because the evidence folder that holds only corrections misrepresents every year it summarises, and the appraisal drawn from it inherits the distortion. The culture dividend is larger than the record: the workforce that sees good work NOTICED — in writing, where it counts — supplies more of it; recognition is the cheapest performance intervention the firm owns.</p><p><b>The check-in cadence.</b> The structured minimum between formal touchpoints: the manager's regular one-to-ones (monthly as the working norm for the trade — brief, scheduled, protected: the goal sheet glanced, the obstacles asked after, the notes kept), distinct from daily operational contact (the shift's traffic is not a check-in; the check-in is the fifteen minutes ABOUT the person, not the queue), and feeding the log. The sizing honesty: at eleven people this is the founder's few hours monthly, and it is precisely the founder-bottleneck's antidote in this layer — the check-ins that develop people are the delegation machinery being built, one conversation at a time.</p><p><b>The manager's log.</b> The continuous layer's memory: the dated notes per person (feedback given, recognition recorded, check-in outcomes, the operational reads' notable moments) — kept in the system against the record (the custody rules of module 1 governing visibility — the log is the manager's and HR's, per the permission design), honest (notes written as if the person will read them, because processes exist in which they may — the discipline that keeps logs professional and defensible at once), and SUFFICIENT (the log is the appraisal's raw material: the December meeting that opens a year of dated notes writes itself; the one that opens a blank log writes fiction). The chapter's closing arithmetic, for the manager who calls this bureaucracy: five minutes a week of notes versus a December of archaeology and a grievance risk — the trade this academy has priced in every layer, at its most human scale.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Underperformance: the governed response", "13", "<p>The layer must also handle the hard case — the performance that persistently misses — and handle it as process, not improvisation. The chapter where fairness and firmness are the same discipline.</p><p><b>Capability versus conduct: the first distinction.</b> The fork every case meets first, because the processes differ: CAPABILITY (the person trying and missing — skill, aptitude, or circumstance gaps: a performance-improvement matter, this chapter's terrain) versus CONDUCT (the person breaching rules — the lateness pattern refused, the policy violated, the integrity matter: the disciplinary machinery, module 7's terrain, with its own procedural requirements). The distinction matters legally and humanly — the capability case gets support and time; the conduct case gets the disciplinary procedure's protections — and the confused case (the capability gap prosecuted as misconduct, or the misconduct excused as a training need) fails both tests at once. The consultant's rule for managers: name which it is, in writing, before any process starts — and where genuinely mixed, run the processes as the distinct tracks they are.</p><p><b>The improvement frame.</b> The capability response, structured (the PIP shape at consultant altitude — the adviser confirms the firm's procedural specifics): the GAP named in evidence (the goal sheet's specific shortfall, the records cited — not 'attitude', but the count accuracy at 91% against the 98% standard), the SUPPORT committed (the retraining, the mentor, the adjusted load — the plan that offers nothing is a dismissal letter with a waiting period, and tribunals read it as exactly that), the TIMELINE set (long enough to be real, bounded enough to be serious — the common eight-to-twelve-week shape), the REVIEWS held on their dates (the progress documented each time — the improvement plan that is never reviewed is theatre in both directions), and the OUTCOMES honest: improvement confirmed and the plan closed with its recognition (the person who climbed back deserves the note as much as the star — Chapter 6's positive record at its most meaningful), or the gap persisting and the process concluding as the employment framework provides (module 7's separation machinery, entered with a file that shows the firm tried — which is simultaneously the legal protection, the ethical position, and the culture signal to everyone watching, because everyone IS watching how the firm treats its strugglers).</p><p><b>The dignity discipline.</b> The frame's conduct rules, because the process is a person's hardest season: confidentiality held (the improvement plan is not branch gossip — module 1's custody at its most pointed), the tone developmental (the meetings about the work, not the worth), the person heard (their account of the gap may contain the fix — the equipment, the rota, the training miss nobody made), and the manager supported too (the first PIP a manager runs needs HR beside them — the process failing on procedure helps no one, least of all the person it concerns).</p><p><b>The system read.</b> The layer's aggregate honesty, closing the chapter: improvement cases read at altitude (the cluster under one manager is a management question; the cluster in one role is a design question — the role that everyone fails was mis-scoped or mis-hired, and module 2's pipeline wants that finding; the cluster after a change is the change's bill arriving) — because a firm's underperformance pattern is diagnostic BEFORE it is disciplinary, and the consultant who reads it that way fixes systems where others cycle through people. The chapter's law in one line: <b>the response to persistent underperformance is a process with support, evidence, and an honest end — never a surprise, never a mood, never a corridor.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's first performance year", "16", "<p>The performance layer, run through its first full cycle. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The goal sheets (Ch. 2).</b> January's setting conversations: one page per person, three to five goals each, evidence sources named. The till roles carry session accuracy and clean-close streaks; the storekeepers carry receiving discipline and count accuracy; the branch managers carry their scorecards' vitals plus one development goal each; the accountant carries the close's day-four target and residue ages. The firm's year (the Akure ramp, the shrinkage band) cascades as context — the branch teams pointing at scorecards already published, nobody carrying goals they cannot touch. The sheets signed, the sources understood: <i>your sessions will be read — that is the deal</i>, said in January, resented by no one in December.</p><p><b>The year between (Ch. 6).</b> The monthly check-ins hold — imperfectly at first (two managers lapse in Q2; the HR nudge and the MD's modelling restore the cadence), then habitually. The logs accumulate: the recognition notes land (the Ibadan storekeeper's clean cycle-count quarter; the till assistant's handling of the disputed-price incident — the selling track's scenario, lived and noted), and the early corrections do their cheap work (a receiving shortcut named in week one at Akure — the two-minute conversation, the habit adjusted, the December that never hears of it because it ended in February).</p><p><b>The cycle runs (Ch. 3-4).</b> The mid-year checks amend three goal sheets honestly (the year changed — the clinic line's launch redrew one role). December's season: self-assessments first (two perception gaps surface early and get their conversations before the meetings), evidence folders opened (the quarterly snapshots outvoting recency — one manager's draft, leaning on a strong November, revises against the spring's records), and the CALIBRATION session earns its chapter: the Lagos manager's distribution sits visibly above the others'; the anchors are re-read, three ratings defend themselves from folders and two revise — the leniency caught before it reached a single meeting, the manager coached, not scolded (<i>'meets' is an honourable rating here — your solid people deserve a scale that says so</i>).</p><p><b>The hard case and the promotion (Ch. 5, 7).</b> One improvement case: a warehouse assistant's count accuracy persistently under standard — named in evidence (91% against 98%), supported (retraining plus a fortnight paired with the Ibadan storekeeper), timelined at ten weeks, reviewed on dates — and CLOSED with improvement confirmed at 97.5%, the recognition note filed (<i>the climb-back documented as deliberately as the gap</i>). And one promotion case built entirely from records: the Akure storekeeper — hired by module 2's practical (the candidate who caught the short), confirmed on evidence, a year of clean receiving and two caught discrepancies in the log — proposed for senior storekeeper with a file that argues itself: the panel reads for ten minutes and approves. The MD's season note joins the collection: <i>we promoted from a folder, not a feeling — and everyone could see why.</i> The layer's first year ends with ratings people trust, development plans feeding module 6's machinery, and the thesis demonstrated: the conversation happened all year; the record just proved it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "13", "<p>The performance layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the annual ambush.</b> Symptom: ratings that surprise; weaknesses first named in December; grievances filed the week after appraisal season. Disease: the record without the conversation (Ch. 1, 6) — the continuous half missing. Fix: feedback near events, the check-in cadence, the manager's log — and the door test enforced: if any line of the form is news, the meeting is a year late.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — performance theatre.</b> Symptom: forms completed, filed, never referenced; ratings from a fortnight's memory; the season costing days and changing nothing. Disease: ritual without a reader (Ch. 1) — the record without consequence. Fix: appraisals wired to their consumers (the development plans feeding module 6, the ratings feeding decisions with written rationales), the evidence folder replacing memory, and the cycle's outputs READ — or the cycle honestly retired, because theatre is worse than nothing: it teaches that records lie.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the everyone-outstanding team.</b> Symptom: uniform top ratings; the genuine star invisible; the eventual honest conversation legally awkward against years of inflated files. Disease: leniency — kindness in the moment, cruelty at the system level (Ch. 5). Fix: anchored scales with 'meets' defended as honourable, the calibration session with evidence talking, the distribution read as questions — and the manager coached that inflation is the ambush's co-author.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the recency appraisal.</b> Symptom: years rated by their final six weeks; strong springs erased by weak Novembers and vice versa. Disease: memory doing evidence's job (Ch. 3). Fix: the evidence folder with dated, year-spanning records; quarterly snapshots filed; disagreements resolved by opening records — the drill-before-disputing culture at the appraisal table.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — capability confused with conduct.</b> Symptom: the struggling worker prosecuted as a misconduct case, or the rule-breaker excused into endless retraining; processes that fail both tests. Disease: the fork never named (Ch. 7). Fix: the written designation before any process starts, the improvement frame for capability with real support, the disciplinary machinery for conduct with its protections — and mixed cases run as the distinct tracks they are.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — promotion by proximity.</b> Symptom: the person the owner sees most rising fastest; the branch's quiet excellence passed over; promotion announcements that surprise and demoralise. Disease: decisions from impressions in a layer built for evidence (Ch. 1, 8) — the founder-bottleneck breeding its succession. Fix: promotion cases built from folders (the goal sheets, the ratings with rationales, the logs), argued to a panel where scale permits, and the standard visible — the workforce that sees folders decide supplies its ambition to the folder, which is exactly the behaviour the firm wants.</p><p><b>The fifth law.</b> The record, the pipeline, the capture, the entitlement — and now the contribution: <b>performance is a conversation with a record — goals few, observable, and shared from January; evidence accumulated by the machinery the firm already runs; feedback near events and recognition in writing; ratings anchored, calibrated, and explained; underperformance met with process and support; and nothing in any appraisal ever news.</b> The conversation develops the person; the record makes it fair, and each keeps the other honest. Module 6 takes the appraisal's forward half — the development plan — and builds the machinery that delivers it: training, by design.</p>"),
]

